    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The southern kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a> for about 340 years.</li><li>It survived as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, resisting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>’s invasion of 701 BCE.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and its temple in 587 BCE; the exile shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel">Siloam tunnel</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, over 500 metres long, was dug from both ends; an inscription records the moment the two teams of diggers met.</p></aside>
    </section>

