    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon.jpg" alt="Solomon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simeon_Solomon,_King_Solomon,_1872_or_1874,_NGA_76152_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simeon Solomon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> emerged around 930 BCE, when the realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> split after his death. According to the books of Kings, the northern tribes rejected Solomon’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rehoboam">Rehoboam</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam</a> their king, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, while the tribe of Judah, with much of Benjamin, stayed loyal to Rehoboam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>. Judah’s territory covered the southern highlands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, the low hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shephelah">Shephelah</a> to the west and the arid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> to the south. It was smaller, poorer and more isolated than its northern neighbour, which controlled the richer valleys and the trade routes of the north. Its great asset was Jerusalem, the dynastic capital, with the royal palace and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> built by Solomon. A few years after the division, around 925 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a>, the biblical Shishak, campaigned in the region; the Bible says Rehoboam bought him off with the treasures of the temple and palace. Archaeologists debate how developed the kingdom was in its first century; many argue that Judah became a fully organised state, with writing, administration and fortified towns, only in the ninth and eighth centuries BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arad-romania"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arad-romania.jpg" alt="Arad, Romania" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arad-romania">Arad, Romania</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arad_(30130671351).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was an agricultural society of villages and small towns. Its farmers grew wheat and barley in the valleys and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shephelah">Shephelah</a>, and olives and vines on terraced hillsides, while herders kept sheep and goats on the desert margins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">Olive oil</a> and wine were the main surpluses, and the late seventh-century industrial zone at Ekron, just across the border in Philistia, processed oil on a massive scale, partly from the Judahite hinterland. Thousands of storage jar handles stamped <em>lmlk</em>, “belonging to the king”, with the names of four towns, show royal collection of produce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>’s time. Trade routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, and fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arad-romania">Arad</a> guarded them. The population grew sharply in the late eighth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> expanded to perhaps 60 hectares, partly through refugees from the north after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>. Society was organised around households and clans, with a royal and priestly elite in Jerusalem. Religion centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, but archaeology reveals widespread household cult, figurines and local shrines, and inscriptions such as those from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khirbet-el-qom">Khirbet el-Qom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuntillet-ajrud">Kuntillet Ajrud</a> mention Yahweh and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>. Prophets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micah-prophet">Micah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> criticised social injustice and religious practice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat.jpg" alt="Jehoshaphat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josaphat_rex.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guillaume Rouille, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>’s army was small, relying on infantry levies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and a modest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> force, and the kingdom defended itself mainly through fortified cities. Lachish, its second city, was surrounded by massive walls and a great gate complex, and fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arad-romania">Arad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azekah">Azekah</a> guarded the frontiers. Early wars were fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, with which Judah alternated between conflict and alliance; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a> fought beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab of Israel</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amaziah-of-judah">Amaziah</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoash-of-israel">Jehoash of Israel</a>, who broke down part of the wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Judah also lost control of Edom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-judah">Jehoram</a> in the mid-ninth century. The decisive military events came with the great empires. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, he strengthened Jerusalem with a broad wall and dug the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel">Siloam tunnel</a> to bring water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gihon-spring">Gihon</a> spring inside the city. In 701 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> invaded, and his annals claim the capture of forty-six walled towns and the shutting up of Hezekiah in Jerusalem “like a bird in a cage”. The siege of Lachish, depicted on reliefs in Sennacherib’s palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, left a siege ramp still visible today. Jerusalem survived, but Judah later fell to the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, who besieged the city in 597 and again in 588–587 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy.jpg" alt="Book of Deuteronomy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy">Book of Deuteronomy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pap_266.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> was ruled throughout by kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>, an unusual stability compared with the frequent coups in the northern kingdom; the only break was the six-year rule of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>, daughter of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, which ended when priests placed her grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joash-of-judah">Joash</a> on the throne. Royal authority was supported by officials such as the steward of the palace, scribes and military commanders, and seals and seal impressions bearing names and titles reveal an expanding bureaucracy in the late eighth and seventh centuries BCE. The king also controlled the temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and royal religious policy shaped the cult. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> is credited with a first reform, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> (c. 640–609 BCE), after a book of the law was found in the temple in 622 BCE, centralised worship in Jerusalem, destroyed local shrines and celebrated a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>. Scholars widely link this book to an early form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy">Deuteronomy</a>. Judah was repeatedly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> around 734 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasseh-of-judah">Manasseh</a>, who ruled for about half a century, remained a loyal Assyrian tributary. After Josiah’s death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiakim">Jehoiakim</a> served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and the last kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiachin">Jehoiachin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zedekiah">Zedekiah</a>, were installed and deposed by foreign powers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible.jpg" alt="Hebrew Bible" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LGLou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>’s influence far exceeded its size because much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> took shape there. Scribes at the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> are widely believed to have collected and edited earlier traditions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deuteronomistic-history">Deuteronomistic History</a>, the account running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-joshua">Joshua</a> to Kings, interprets Israel’s past in light of Josiah’s reform and the later disaster of exile. The prophets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micah-prophet">Micah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> and others, whose words were preserved and expanded in Judah and in exile, developed ideas of justice, covenant and the sole worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> that shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and, through it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The name Judah gave rise to the words Judaean and Jew. Inscriptions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel">Siloam tunnel</a> inscription, the Lachish letters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arad-romania">Arad</a> ostraca give rare first-hand glimpses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> writing and administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> amulets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ketef-hinnom">Ketef Hinnom</a>, probably of the late seventh or early sixth century BCE, carry a version of the priestly blessing, among the oldest known texts paralleling the Bible. The idea that a dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> would be restored became the root of messianic expectation, and the destruction of the First Temple, commemorated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisha-bav">Tisha B’Av</a>, remains a defining memory in Jewish tradition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish.jpg" alt="Battle of Carchemish" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish">Battle of Carchemish</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Carchemish.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Egisto C., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>’s end came in the struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. In 609 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> was killed at Megiddo confronting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a>, who then installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiakim">Jehoiakim</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish">Battle of Carchemish</a> in 605 BCE, Jehoiakim submitted to Babylon but later rebelled. Nebuchadnezzar besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a> records that he captured it in March 597 BCE, deported the young king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiachin">Jehoiachin</a> and installed his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zedekiah">Zedekiah</a>. Tablets from Babylon list rations for Jehoiachin and his sons in captivity. Zedekiah too rebelled, encouraged by Egypt, and the Babylonian army returned. The Lachish letters, written by an officer to his commander, describe the final campaign, one noting that the signals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azekah">Azekah</a> could no longer be seen. Jerusalem fell after a long siege in 587 BCE, or by another reckoning 586 BCE; the temple and city were burned, Zedekiah was blinded and taken to Babylon, and much of the elite was deported. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedaliah">Gedaliah</a> governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mizpah-in-benjamin">Mizpah</a>, but he was assassinated. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Babylon in 539 BCE, exiles began to return to the Persian province of Yehud Medinata.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a> for about 340 years, survived Assyrian and Egyptian domination before falling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 587 BCE, and the traditions, prophecy and history written there and in exile became the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>.</em></p>

